When you see google:suggesttype in a Google Autocomplete HTTP response (typically when using client=chrome), it is a metadata array that categorizes each suggestion. It tells the client what kind of data was used to generate that specific prediction.While Google does not provide an official public legend for these internal codes, technical analysis of the responses reveals the following common values:Common google:suggesttype ValuesValueMeaningDescriptionQUERYStandard SearchA general keyword suggestion based on popular searches across Google.NAVIGATIONWebsite/URLA suggestion that points directly to a website (e.g., typing "face" might suggest "facebook.com").PERSONALIZEDYour HistoryA result based specifically on your own past search history (usually requires being logged in).CALCULATORMath/ToolTriggers for math queries (e.g., "5+5") or unit conversions.ENTITYKnowledge GraphRefers to a specific person, place, or thing known to Google’s Knowledge Graph.TAILSearch CompletionA specific type of "long-tail" completion often appended to the end of your current string.


